!!schema:"https://tson.io/2026/35/ltr8/http/deployment-1.tn"
@doc:"""
  The validator demo's own deployment descriptor.

  It is NOT published and NOT resolvable: no schema imports it, no data document names it, and the catalog
  never serves it. What a caller may see is the projection at /.well-known/tson-deployment, derived from this
  by TsonDeployment.profile(). That is deployment-1.tn's rule 2, and this file is where it would be broken if
  it were going to be.

  `tokens` is set to something visibly strict so the demo demonstrates a deployment decision rather than
  describing one: submit a document with a mixed-script value and the verdict changes. Take it out and the
  same document conforms. That is the whole argument for the artifact, in a form you can run.

  `identifiers` is deliberately unstated, which leaves declared names at the library's default of Highly
  Restrictive over the whole name. An absent policy is not a permissive one.

  Note where the annotations sit. [TSON-DATA] §2.5 admits no directive, annotation or type annotation before a
  FIELD NAME -- metadata about a field attaches to the field's VALUE. Writing `@doc:"..." tokens: {...}` is a
  parse error, and it is the natural thing to reach for after writing a few schemas, where the entry position
  takes one.
  """
!deployment {
  name: "validator-demo"

  listener: { port: 8080 }

  tokens: @doc:"""
      Stricter than the default, which is unrestricted. A service that renders or matches what it reads faces
      on values the spoofing surface [TSON-DATA] §9.4 raises for names, and §8.2 says such a deployment
      applies a level knowingly. SINGLE_SCRIPT refuses a token that mixes scripts: a wholly Cyrillic display
      name is fine, and a Latin word with one Cyrillic letter in it is not.
      """
    { level: SINGLE_SCRIPT }

  limits: @doc:"""
      [TSON-DATA] §9.1's resource limits, stated at the library's own default rather than left absent. A
      validator is exactly the service that should bound what it reads -- both the schema and the document
      come from the request -- so the posture is worth writing down even where it changes nothing.

      64 and not less, deliberately. This demo is a conformance tool: it runs the same scenarios the
      TypeScript implementation runs in a browser so the two verdicts can be compared, and a bound tighter
      than §9.1's default would make them disagree about a deep document for a reason that is this
      deployment's and not the format's.
      """
    { max_depth: 64 }

  schema_hosts: @doc:"Empty, and a real posture: this service resolves only the schema sent with a request." []
}
